  <h1>Jamaican Jerk Seasoning</h1>

  <div class="answer-box">
    <p>Jamaican jerk seasoning is a bold Caribbean spice blend built on <strong>allspice</strong> (Jamaicans call it pimento), fiery <strong>scotch bonnet</strong> peppers, and fresh <strong>thyme</strong>, rounded out with warm spices like nutmeg and cinnamon. It comes two ways — a dry rub or a wet paste — and it's what gives jerk chicken that smoky, sweet-hot, unmistakable punch.</p>
  </div>

  <ContentImage src="/images/jamaican-jerk-seasoning.webp" alt="Jamaican jerk seasoning dry rub in a wooden bowl surrounded by fresh spices" eager={true} />

  <p>Here's the thing about jerk seasoning: it does not mess around. The first time I made it from scratch I got a little enthusiastic with the scotch bonnet, and let's just say my tasting spoon and I needed a short timeout and a tall glass of milk. <em>[Switches to serious face]</em> But dialed in right, it's one of the great flavor bombs in all of cooking — warm, sweet, and hot at the same time. This is your complete guide: how to make it, what's actually in it, dry versus wet, how the famous store brands stack up, and how to keep your mouth off the ceiling.</p>

  <h2>Jamaican Jerk Seasoning Recipe</h2>
  <p>This is the base <strong>jamaican jerk seasoning recipe</strong>, and I have good news for the nervous cooks in the room: it's a "dump everything in a bowl and whisk" situation. If you can make a decent bowl of cereal, you can make this. The hard part is resisting the urge to sniff the allspice too closely (you will sneeze — it's a rite of passage).</p>

  <RecipeCardBlock
    title={recipe.name}
    image={recipe.image}
    description={recipe.description}
    prepTime={recipe.prepTime}
    cookTime={recipe.cookTime}
